  "slug": "related-disorder-models-ad7b",
  "summary": "Independent TTC5 mouse studies and variant-specific dynein organoids tie progenitor mitosis and cytoskeletal regulation to cortical malformation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is disease-variant models that trace developmental malformation to progenitor-level cytoskeletal and mitotic defects. Two Ttc5 loss-of-function mouse studies, one a knockout and one a patient nonsense mutation, converge on impaired neurogenesis, mitotic errors and disrupted tubulin homeostasis, with elevated γ-tubulin extending tubulin autoregulation to TTC5. Patient-derived cortical organoids show that dynein variants differ in their effects on spindle organization and apoptosis. A catalytically inactive TAOK1 variant in organoids enhances progenitor proliferation via impaired Hippo signaling and YAP1. Two items are related but distinct: p53-dependent mesenchymal progenitor defects in a Diamond Blackfan anemia model, and a Hippo-dystrophin axis maintaining cortical actin in Capsaspora. New here are the convergent TTC5 evidence and the variant-specific dynein comparison.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
